    "done_when": "Every reckon crew dispatch is a fresh CLI process. Its picker step (dispatch_picker_selection in reckon/crew/dispatch.py, with a 5 s bound) is therefore always a cold pick. A peer node measured a real fresh-process, dispatch-path pick for project reckon at about 9.8 s and 9.7 s, dominated by candidate context measurement; the Jev call itself takes 302–554 ms. Since 2026-10-03T03:00Z, recorded picks have timed out at the bound and fallen back to the flight default lane, so Jev is often not choosing. The in-process memoisation added earlier helps only a second pick in the same process.\n\n1. Profile the fresh-process path phase by phase on this workstation, with three runs each, cold and back-to-back. Use a small driver that runs dispatch_picker_selection in a new python process exactly as `reckon crew dispatch` reaches it. Attribute wall time at least to these phases: candidate snapshot; each candidate's competence and context verdict (routing._competence_verdict, routing._context_fit_verdict, and whatever estimates the node's context: the instruction-chain read and its git subprocesses); routing.shared_verdict_inputs, meaning capabilities.load_capabilities and capabilities.project_cache_status; the budget snapshot; run_time_profile and local-lane load; template render; the Jev call. Record the table in docs/evidence/fragments/one-typesafe-model-picker/picker-fresh-process-pick.html, with figures from tool output only.\n2. Cut the dominant fresh-process costs so that a warm-cache fresh-process pick has a median at or below 2.5 s, with the cold first pick also under the 5 s bound on this GPFS. A warm-cache pick means the second process after the first. A cross-process cache is allowed for a measured input that is a pure function of files: a per-input file under ~/.cache/reckon/, resolved through an environment-overridable path so tests use a temp dir. Key it on a cheap stamp of those files, the mtime and size of each file the input reads, or the HEAD of the repository it reads. Write it atomically. A corrupt or unreadable cache is a miss, never an error. Never cache anything that must be live: availability, lane load, budget pace. No pick may come out different from an uncached pick; prove this with a test comparing the cached and uncached selection inputs on the same fixture.\n3. Do not edit reckon/crew/picker/lane_context.py or reckon/crew/run_time_profile.py, which a concurrent node owns, or reckon/crew/dispatch.py, where a concurrent node changes the dispatch() pick call site. Pass anything those files would need as a follow-on.\n\nTests: tests/test_picker_fresh_process_pick.py. It covers the equivalence of cached and uncached inputs, a stale stamp causing a recompute, a corrupt cache file being a miss, and a two-subprocess run in which the second process skips the cached computation, counted through a spy written to a temp file. Gate: that file and tests/test_picker*.py, run with the main checkout's .venv/bin/python -m pytest -p no:cacheprovider, with one log for base and one for head under the run directory, judged by delta. The coordinator runs the dispatch population at merge.",
    "estimated_hours": null,
    "goal": "A fresh-process dispatch pick finishes inside its 5 s bound",
